{"id":195419295104828,"type":"album","name":"Cop","artist":"Swans","year":1984,"weight":29,"info":{"cover":"https://dn721609.ca.archive.org/0/items/mbid-1af7f2cc-2c27-407f-b0a4-28de2ba0512b/mbid-1af7f2cc-2c27-407f-b0a4-28de2ba0512b-27701035373.jpg","kind":"Album","tracks":8,"country":"GB","scaruffi":8.0,"yt":"t7Dp9WNgX08","links":{"youtube":"https://music.youtube.com/playlist?list=OLAK5uy_mHHO09Oh9svzXiDgFROEGdEYp0Lz9oVC4","bandcamp":"https://swans.bandcamp.com/album/cop-young-god","rateyourmusic":"https://rateyourmusic.com/release/album/swans/cop"},"desc":"\"Cop\" is the second studio album by American experimental rock band Swans. It was released in 1984, through record label K.422. On \"Cop\", Swans took the style of their previous LP, 1983's \"Filth\" and intensified it, utilising slower tempos, more tape loops and even more abrasive musical textures. The lyrics are again concerned with ambiguous themes like physical, often sexual domination and/or submission. According to Jarboe, who first met the band shortly after this album was recorded, the photograph on the cover is the profile of a morbidly obese woman. \"Cop\" was remastered by Michael Gira in 1992 for release ."},"artist_ref":{"id":188548856103507,"name":"Swans","info":{"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Swans_warsaw_10_12_2010_poland_m_kutera.jpg?width=500"}},"labels":[],"genres":[{"id":274490571464652,"type":"genre","name":"Noise Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":360,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":269233294035886,"type":"genre","name":"No Wave","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":332,"yt":null,"src":"musthear,rym"},{"id":67015852974648,"type":"genre","name":"Sludge Metal","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":237,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":217093372136338,"type":"genre","name":"Industrial Metal","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":231,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":53692558339894,"type":"genre","name":"experimental","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":63,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":7314038540357,"type":"genre","name":"Industrial Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":267,"yt":null,"src":"rym"}],"lists":[{"id":181172873420596,"name":"RYM Top — Scenes & Movements — No Wave","src":"musthear","grp":"rateyourmusic","list_year":null,"rank":21,"year":null,"note":null,"total":98,"scaruffi":8.0},{"id":52032266791182,"name":"Scaruffi 1980s","src":"musthear","grp":null,"list_year":null,"rank":104,"year":null,"note":null,"total":877,"scaruffi":8.0},{"id":224896751673336,"name":"RYM Top — Rock — Industrial Rock","src":"musthear","grp":"rateyourmusic","list_year":null,"rank":107,"year":null,"note":null,"total":200,"scaruffi":8.0}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"Released very close to *Filth*, this album shares its characteristics as an early Swans work. It is now considered a masterpiece of experimental guitar sound, despite having almost no repercussion, low sales, and receiving very bad reviews upon release. The album featured Michael Gira's pioneering atmospheric guitar sound and disturbing lyrics that explored themes of sex, religion, cruelty, submission, and aggressive behaviors. Its radical departure from contemporary rock meant it was largely unknown and unappreciated at the time. \"Hoy en día esos dos trabajos están considerados, obras maestras de la experimentación del sonido de guitarra.\"","year":1983,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Music Radar Clan","episode":"SWANS. Una visión del rock diferente","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sJ3HuN7woDc"}]}],"book_tracks":[{"id":195419295104828,"artist":"Swans","artist_id":188548856103507,"title":"Cop","year":1984,"ess":1,"yt":"QshAx6hEDpo","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Minimal-rock: Sonic Youth y Swans","scene_id":179922132646505,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#minimal-rock","chapters":["Sección 5.13"],"pages":[[1,1